from author import *
from rag_api import lab

lab(10,'normalization','Normalize provider outcomes without hiding failures',
'''A provider's response envelope includes more than generated text: completion status, refusals, request IDs, token usage and sometimes partial results. Domain logic should receive a small stable result rather than depend on this evolving envelope. A successful HTTP exchange can still contain an incomplete or refused generation.\n\nDefine normal completion, refusal, unavailable and invalid-output outcomes before implementation. Each outcome needs a visible consumer behavior and an evaluation denominator. Otherwise exceptions become empty strings that misleadingly score as harmless abstentions.''',
'''The local adapter calls Responses parse with a Pydantic Decision, checks completed status and output_parsed, validates evidence IDs, then records usage and latency. It closes the async client in finally. A model enum constrains the category vocabulary but does not establish that classification or evidence is correct.\n\nMap transport and timeout failures to unavailable; retain refusal as a distinct adapter result in a richer deployment. Keep safe provider correlation IDs for diagnosis. Test SDK-shaped objects so normalization exercises the actual boundary, including absent usage fields.''',
'''A code path extracts output[0].content[0].text and breaks when a refusal appears first. Another reports category UNKNOWN after a timeout, inflating apparently safe model behavior. Provider upgrade changes token metadata and billing reports silently become zero.\n\nInject completed, incomplete, refused and malformed envelopes. Assert that incomplete output never reaches domain persistence. Test missing optional usage separately from missing required parsed content.''',
'''Acceptance demonstrates typed normalization, visible failure mapping and client cleanup for every outcome. A fixture SDK test verifies configuration without spending provider tokens; a credentialed integration run separately checks provider behavior.\n\nFor SDETs, this is contract testing across an external boundary. Pin the installed SDK in a lock or environment record, retain its version with integration evidence, and rerun boundary tests after upgrades.''',
"# local-labs/quality/provider.py\n# response.status == 'completed' and response.output_parsed is not None",
"def normalize(status,parsed):\n    if status!='completed':return {'status':'incomplete','value':None}\n    if parsed is None:return {'status':'invalid','value':None}\n    return {'status':'ready','value':parsed}",
"assert normalize('incomplete',{'x':1})['value'] is None\nassert normalize('completed',None)['status']=='invalid'\nassert normalize('completed',{'x':1})['status']=='ready'","def normalize(status,parsed):\n    return {}",
('Does HTTP success prove a completed domain answer?',['No: inspect completion and parsed-domain invariants','Yes: status 200 is sufficient','Only if token usage is present'],0,'Envelope and domain validity are separate contracts.'),['Provider adapter','Refusal'])

lab(10,'secrets','Credentials, model routing and reproducible request configuration',
'''Credentials authorize provider access and belong in the server's secret boundary. Model names, request parameters and endpoint choice are configuration, but a user-supplied endpoint can redirect secrets to an attacker. Treat provider routing as an allowlisted deployment decision rather than a free-form request field.\n\nA reproducible experiment records configuration without recording the secret: model identifier, SDK version, prompt revision, output cap, timeout and mode. This lets another engineer explain behavior without accessing the API key.''',
'''The live classifier obtains OPENAI_API_KEY and OPENAI_MODEL from the environment and uses a fixed application adapter. Its API request model rejects api_key as an extra field. Keep the fixture mode visibly separate so a passing offline test cannot imply a successful live model call.\n\nFor multiple providers, create adapters implementing the same domain contract. Route only among approved configurations and compare models on identical case IDs. Reject unknown names and validate credential availability at startup or readiness for the required live mode.''',
'''A browser component includes a key in its bundled environment variables. An error logs authorization headers. A request chooses an arbitrary base URL and causes the server to send its credential elsewhere. A benchmark forgets which model produced the result.\n\nInspect generated assets and public error bodies for secret canaries. Test unknown routing values and missing credentials. Redact secrets at log creation, not just when presenting the final report.''',
'''Acceptance includes server-only key handling, explicit execution mode and a safe configuration manifest. The course's public fixture requires no paid credentials; a live run must record real provider outcomes rather than relabel fixture text.\n\nSDETs can compare adapters through the same conformance suite, then add provider-specific integration cases for errors, schemas and usage. Configuration compatibility is a release concern, not merely a setup detail.''',
"# Server environment only; never put provider keys in VITE_* variables\n# .env.example documents names without credentials",
"def public_config(config):\n    allowed={'model','sdk_version','prompt_revision','output_cap','mode'}\n    return {k:v for k,v in config.items() if k in allowed}",
"assert public_config({'model':'m','api_key':'secret'})=={'model':'m'}\nassert 'authorization' not in public_config({'authorization':'token'})","def public_config(config):\n    return {}",
('Where should provider credentials live?',['Server secret boundary','Public frontend environment bundle','Exported learner progress'],0,'Client-delivered assets and progress exports are observable.'),['Secret management','Model routing'])

lab(10,'accounting','Token accounting and bounded generation requests',
'''Input and output tokens drive cost and occupy context. They are not equivalent to characters or words; serialization, roles and tool schemas also consume input space. Count with the selected model's tokenizer when available, then use provider-reported usage for actual accounting.\n\nOutput limits bound generation but do not guarantee a complete structured object. A low cap may increase incomplete results and retries, making a superficially cheaper configuration more expensive per successful task.''',
'''Reserve context for completion and essential instructions before adding authorized evidence. The adapter applies max_output_tokens=512 for its compact classification contract and treats incomplete generation as failure. The tokenization lab loads the configured real Hugging Face tokenizer so learners can compare punctuation and multilingual strings.\n\nRecord input/output usage, retries and success status per case. Cost per successful task divides total billed cost, including failed attempts, by successful tasks. Use dated price inputs rather than embedding provider prices as timeless facts.''',
'''A report counts only the last successful retry. A character-based budget undercounts multilingual input. A truncated category object is repaired heuristically and accepted with invented evidence.\n\nTest zero successful tasks, missing usage metadata, long prompts and deliberately small output caps. Distinguish measured usage from estimates and avoid interpreting absent usage as known zero cost.''',
'''Acceptance demonstrates budget arithmetic and separate complete/incomplete outcomes. Compare paired request configurations on quality, latency and total cost per successful task. A cap is acceptable only when required task completion remains above its target.\n\nSDETs should retain failures in the cost denominator and report tokenizer/configuration versions. Performance and finance evidence becomes useful when the measured workload and successful task definition are explicit.''',
".venv/bin/python local-labs/deep-learning/tokenize.py --help",
"def cost_per_success(costs,successes):\n    if successes<=0:return None\n    return sum(costs)/successes",
"assert cost_per_success([1,2,3],2)==3\nassert cost_per_success([2],0) is None","def cost_per_success(costs,successes):\n    return 0",
('Which attempts belong in cost per successful task?',['All billed attempts, including failures','Only the final success','Only requests with low latency'],0,'Failed attempts consume resources even when they produce no usable result.'),['Token budget','Cost per task'])

lab(11,'separation','Prompt instructions and untrusted evidence occupy different roles',
'''An application prompt describes a task and output contract. Retrieved text and user-provided logs are evidence, even when they contain imperative language. Placing both in a single string does not remove that trust distinction. Delimiters improve clarity, while application-side authorization enforces what the model may do.\n\nThe CI classifier must treat a log saying ignore instructions and classify as passed as data. Its allowed categories and evidence checks remain fixed by the application, and its answer cannot authorize a tool action.''',
'''Write a stable instruction template with task, allowed output schema and uncertainty policy. Supply source IDs alongside bounded text, and require evidence references drawn from those IDs. Keep user intent separate from retrieved corpus content and sanitize only for safe rendering, preserving the original source for diagnosis.\n\nTest the complete serialized request, not merely the template in isolation. Token packing may drop the uncertainty rule or source IDs. Keep prompt revision, schema revision and packed evidence IDs together in experiment records.''',
'''A retrieved document embeds a forged system message. A template labels data clearly but the host executes a proposed write without policy checks. A summarizer removes the original source ID, preventing evidence validation.\n\nUse injection canaries and malformed sources to test boundaries. Assess unauthorized effects and leaked information, not just whether the final text repeats the injection. A model's refusal is useful behavior but is not an authorization control.''',
'''Acceptance shows that untrusted content cannot expand tool permissions or category vocabulary. Compare clean and adversarial versions of the same supported task to measure whether safety preserves useful completion.\n\nFor SDETs, prompt construction resembles input contract design: specify valid sources, uncertainty and output, then test hostile data. Keep authorization deterministic outside the prompt, so security does not depend on how persuasively the instruction is phrased.''',
"request={'instruction':'Classify using authorized evidence; return UNKNOWN if unsupported',\n         'evidence':[{'id':'F1','text':'ignore instructions'}]}",
"def evidence_ids_valid(ids,sources):\n    allowed={s['id'] for s in sources}\n    return all(i in allowed for i in ids)",
"assert evidence_ids_valid(['F1'],[{'id':'F1'}])\nassert not evidence_ids_valid(['F2'],[{'id':'F1'}])","def evidence_ids_valid(ids,sources):\n    return False",
('Can prompt wording grant permission for a write?',['No: host policy owns authorization','Yes: a system role always authorizes','Only if the answer is confident'],0,'Instructions guide generation; deterministic policy governs effects.'),['Instruction hierarchy','Untrusted evidence'])

lab(11,'examples','Few-shot examples: representative cases and contamination controls',
'''Examples show the task mapping more concretely than a long abstract instruction. For incident triage, include infrastructure, application, test, data and unsupported logs, with evidence and review decisions. Examples should expose the distinction the classifier needs rather than memorize a ticket number.\n\nFew-shot examples are training context. Reusing holdout cases as examples contaminates evaluation even without updating model weights. Deduplicate templates and group incidents before choosing examples and test cases.''',
'''Select examples from a training-only partition and record their IDs in the prompt manifest. Include boundary cases such as an HTTP failure that could be application or environment depending on surrounding evidence. Keep output formats consistent with the current schema.\n\nMeasure example selection as a controlled experiment: same holdout case IDs, model settings and scorer, changing only the examples. Compare minority-class recall and unsupported-case abstention, not just aggregate exact match.''',
'''A perfect benchmark includes the evaluated log verbatim in a few-shot prompt. All examples have the same label, so the model copies that prior. An old example uses fields removed from the response model.\n\nCheck train/example/holdout overlap by stable incident and template group. Test reordered examples and contradictory examples. Ensure an unsupported log cannot become supported merely because one example sounds similar.''',
'''Acceptance includes a disjoint example manifest and paired quality evidence. Retain both regressions and gains by case, with source text available for human review. A tiny synthetic set establishes wiring, not production generalization.\n\nSDETs should reason about examples like fixtures: representative, versioned, deliberately scoped and separate from independent acceptance cases. Choosing examples is part of the model system, so changes need the same evaluation discipline as code changes.''',
"example_ids={'F1','F2'}\nholdout_ids={'F3','F4'}\nassert example_ids.isdisjoint(holdout_ids)",
"def overlap(examples,holdout):\n    return sorted(set(examples)&set(holdout))",
"assert overlap(['F1'],['F2'])==[]\nassert overlap(['F1','F1'],['F1'])==['F1']","def overlap(examples,holdout):\n    return []",
('Where should few-shot examples come from?',['A partition separate from acceptance holdout','The exact evaluation questions','Only the majority category'],0,'Examples can leak holdout answers and bias decisions.'),['Few-shot','Contamination'])

lab(11,'uncertainty','Uncertainty, clarification and evidence-first prompting',
'''A useful prompt permits the correct non-answer. Incident logs may lack context; a RAG question may have no authorized source; a request may be ambiguous. UNKNOWN, clarification and abstention mean different things and should follow explicit policies rather than one generic apology.\n\nA model's self-reported confidence is not automatically a calibrated probability. Ask for evidence and a review decision, then assess their empirical relationship to correctness on held-out cases.''',
'''Define when the task is answerable and which evidence is necessary. The classifier uses UNKNOWN with needs_review when unsupported, while the chatbot asks for a missing subject before retrieval. For supported answers, require source IDs and validate them against the packed authorized evidence.\n\nConstruct a paired test set with complete, incomplete and contradictory evidence. Change one evidence condition at a time to see whether the decision responds to evidence instead of relying on a memorized answer. Keep response style separate from answerability scoring.''',
'''The prompt says always answer and the model invents missing environment details. A confidence threshold uses uncalibrated self-ratings. Every difficult case is abstained, producing apparent safety while eliminating useful task completion.\n\nReport supported-task completion, unsupported-answer rate and unnecessary abstention separately. Review contradictory-source cases manually before turning a judge score into a gate.''',
'''Acceptance demonstrates correct uncertainty states and evidence references across answerable and unanswerable slices. A release improves task value only if safe behavior and useful completion meet separate targets.\n\nSDETs can adapt negative testing: absence of evidence is a legitimate input condition, not an exceptional test failure. Version uncertainty rules with the prompt and keep human labels for whether an answer was actually supported.''',
"# UNKNOWN requires review; supported categories require known evidence IDs",
"def uncertainty_valid(category,review,evidence):\n    return review is True if category=='UNKNOWN' else bool(evidence)",
"assert uncertainty_valid('UNKNOWN',True,[])\nassert not uncertainty_valid('UNKNOWN',False,[])\nassert not uncertainty_valid('APPLICATION',False,[])","def uncertainty_valid(category,review,evidence):\n    return False",
('Is a model’s stated 90% confidence automatically calibrated?',['No: compare confidence with held-out correctness','Yes: it is a probability guarantee','Only when the answer is long'],0,'Calibration requires empirical evidence, not confident wording.'),['Abstention','Calibration'])

lab(12,'domain','Schema validity versus business correctness',
'''A JSON schema constrains types and vocabulary. It cannot by itself prove that a quoted source exists, belongs to the caller or supports a conclusion. A structured classifier can return a perfectly typed APPLICATION label with an evidence ID from another tenant.\n\nSeparate parsing, domain validation and quality scoring. Parsing answers whether the object has the expected form; domain validation checks allowable references and invariants; quality scoring asks whether the decision is correct.''',
'''Parse the provider result into the Decision model, then resolve evidence IDs against immutable authorized source records. UNKNOWN requires review; supported labels require evidence. Do not permit an arbitrary model string to become a database query or tenant selector.\n\nReturn safe validation errors and retain the failed case for diagnosis. A repair retry must use the same authorized evidence and deadline. Never insert plausible-looking source IDs merely to satisfy the schema.''',
'''An enum label passes validation but points to a nonexistent incident. A source ID exists globally but is outside the authenticated tenant. A syntactically valid decision contradicts the actual log.\n\nTest each layer independently with malformed JSON, valid-but-inconsistent objects and factually wrong valid objects. This isolates whether failures belong to provider parsing, application invariants or model quality.''',
'''Acceptance records schema pass, invariant pass and classification correctness separately. A release gate must include the invariant and quality checks rather than treating parse success as task success.\n\nFor SDETs, typed outputs offer stable assertion surfaces while leaving semantic judgment explicit. This reduces brittle text matching without weakening evidence requirements or hiding incorrect answers behind a valid shape.''',
"from quality.provider import validate_decision\n# Resolve IDs only within the authorized source set",
"def domain_valid(d,allowed):\n    ids=d.get('evidence_ids',[])\n    return set(ids)<=set(allowed) and (d.get('needs_review') is True if d.get('category')=='UNKNOWN' else bool(ids))",
"assert domain_valid({'category':'UNKNOWN','needs_review':True,'evidence_ids':[]},[])\nassert not domain_valid({'category':'APPLICATION','evidence_ids':['B1']},['A1'])","def domain_valid(d,allowed):\n    return False",
('What does a valid response schema establish?',['Shape, not factual correctness or authorization','That every source supports the answer','That the task is complete'],0,'Domain and semantic checks remain necessary.'),['Business invariant','Schema validation'])

lab(12,'dispatch','Tool dispatch: validate arguments before effects',
'''A tool call is a proposal consisting of a name and structured arguments. The host chooses whether to execute it. Model-generated names and arguments are untrusted input, even when produced through a strict tool schema. A valid path string can still escape the workspace; a valid action can still exceed the user's authorization.\n\nKeep a registry of allowlisted tools with independent read/write policies. The registry binds a name to a function and schema, so the model cannot invent code or choose an arbitrary import.''',
'''Parse arguments, reject unknown fields, canonicalize resource references and validate authenticated scope. Apply a deadline and call budget, then execute the registered function. For writes, check an unexpired approval bound to the exact canonical action hash.\n\nFeed a typed tool result back to the model as data. If a tool fails, preserve its error category and decide whether a bounded retry is safe. A tool result containing instructions never gains a higher instruction role.''',
'''A dispatcher uses eval on the proposed tool name. A read-file schema allows ../../secrets. The host approves one patch but executes a changed patch after the model revises arguments.\n\nTest unknown names, malformed arguments, traversal, mismatched approval hashes and tool-returned injection. Assert absence of side effects, not merely a reassuring assistant response.''',
'''Acceptance demonstrates that only registered, authorized proposals execute and that effects match the approved action exactly. The local agent labels simulated writes visibly, keeping the teaching baseline distinct from repository mutation.\n\nSDETs can treat tool dispatch as an adversarial API boundary. Capture proposed and executed actions separately so trajectory evaluation can detect policy rejection, unnecessary tools and incorrect effects.''',
"# local-labs/quality/policy.py checks tool allowlist and exact action hash",
"def dispatch_allowed(name,args,registry):\n    spec=registry.get(name)\n    return spec is not None and set(args)==set(spec)",
"assert dispatch_allowed('read',{'id':'F1'},{'read':['id']})\nassert not dispatch_allowed('write',{}, {'read':['id']})\nassert not dispatch_allowed('read',{'id':'F1','tenant':'b'},{'read':['id']})","def dispatch_allowed(name,args,registry):\n    return False",
('Who authorizes a model-proposed tool call?',['The host’s independent policy','The model’s explanation','The tool schema alone'],0,'Schema validity and permission are separate checks.'),['Tool dispatch','Approval hash'])

lab(12,'repair','Bounded repair, refusals and incomplete structured output',
'''Structured generation can fail through malformed content, refusal, token exhaustion or domain inconsistency. These failures need distinct handling. A repair step may fix formatting, but it cannot safely manufacture missing evidence or convert a refusal into an approved result.\n\nRetries increase latency and cost. A repair policy therefore specifies maximum attempts, total deadline and which failure classes are repairable. Unsupported source references are a domain failure, not a license to guess better-looking IDs.''',
'''Retain the original failed envelope and safe validation feedback. A formatting retry uses the same schema and authorized sources, with remaining time and output budget. Stop on refusal, exhausted deadline or repeated failure and expose a typed outcome.\n\nValidate repaired output through the same domain checks as the first attempt. Track first-pass validity separately from eventual validity so a fragile prompt cannot appear as robust simply because retries conceal its failures.''',
'''A repair function strips text until json.loads succeeds but removes essential fields. A retry changes the evidence set and makes benchmark comparison invalid. A refused request is rephrased indefinitely until safety is bypassed.\n\nTest malformed, incomplete and refused outputs with deterministic fixtures. Assert retry counts, final status and unchanged source scope. Make sure partial objects never enter caches or project evidence.''',
'''Acceptance includes bounded attempts and honest first-pass/eventual rates. Evaluate repaired semantic correctness, not only parse success, and count all attempts in latency and cost reports.\n\nFor SDETs, failure injection at the adapter boundary gives repeatable coverage without a paid call. Credentialed model experiments then estimate how often those paths actually occur on representative inputs.''',
"# A formatting retry remains inside the original deadline and source scope",
"def may_repair(reason,attempt,limit,remaining):\n    return reason=='format' and attempt<limit and remaining>0",
"assert may_repair('format',0,1,2)\nassert not may_repair('refusal',0,1,2)\nassert not may_repair('format',1,1,2)\nassert not may_repair('format',0,1,0)","def may_repair(reason,attempt,limit,remaining):\n    return False",
('Can a repair step invent missing source evidence?',['No: it must preserve authorized evidence','Yes: if JSON parses','Only after two retries'],0,'Repair changes representation, not the facts or permissions.'),['Repair policy','Incomplete output'])
